These equal-sized bars are divided into equal parts.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"fractionBlue","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shaded numerator area or marked numerator objects"},{"name":"fractionOrange","hex":"FCE8D5","reason":"Second model numerator area or marked objects"}],"reference":"Source-adapted same-denominator area comparison; light shaded regions mark numerator parts; black outlines/labels"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.65cm]
\definecolor{fractionBlue}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\definecolor{fractionOrange}{HTML}{FCE8D5}
\foreach \i in {0,...,0} {\fill[fractionBlue] (\i,1.7) rectangle (\i+1,2.7);}
\foreach \i in {0,...,4} {\fill[fractionOrange] (\i,0) rectangle (\i+1,1);}
\draw[black] (0,1.7) rectangle (6,2.7);
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (6,1);
\foreach \i in {1,...,5} {\draw[black] (\i,1.7)--(\i,2.7);\draw[black] (\i,0)--(\i,1);}
\node[left,text=black] at (0,2.2) {A};
\node[left,text=black] at (0,0.5) {B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
a. Write and compare the shaded fractions.
b. Why must the whole bars be equal-sized?

The rectangles have equal areas.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"fractionBlue","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shaded numerator area or marked numerator objects"},{"name":"fractionOrange","hex":"FCE8D5","reason":"Second model numerator area or marked objects"}],"reference":"Source-adapted equivalent areas by rectangle subdivision in both directions; standard light fills mathematically mark equal fractional regions"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.1cm,y=0.7cm]
\definecolor{fractionBlue}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\definecolor{fractionOrange}{HTML}{FCE8D5}
\fill[fractionBlue] (0,1.9) rectangle (2,3.9);
\draw[black] (0,1.9) rectangle (3,3.9);
\draw[black] (0,2.9)--(3,2.9);
\foreach \i in {1,2} {\draw[black] (\i,1.9)--(\i,3.9);}
\fill[fractionOrange] (0,-0.9) rectangle (2,1.1);
\draw[black] (0,-0.9) rectangle (3,1.1);
\foreach \i in {1,2} {\draw[black] (\i,-0.9)--(\i,1.1);}
\node[left,text=black] at (0,2.9) {A};
\node[left,text=black] at (0,0.1) {B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Write and compare the shaded fractions.

These equal-sized bars are divided into equal parts. A student says Q has the greater shaded fraction because it has more shaded parts. Use the area models to correct the claim.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"fractionBlue","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shaded numerator area"},{"name":"fractionOrange","hex":"FCE8D5","reason":"Second model numerator area"}],"reference":"Adapted area-model error analysis from Comparing Fractions question 5, lines 1914–1943; equal-whole comparison follows Related Denominators question 1c, lines 2044–2054"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm]
\definecolor{fractionBlue}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\definecolor{fractionOrange}{HTML}{FCE8D5}
\fill[fractionBlue] (0,1.7) rectangle (2,2.7);
\draw[black] (0,1.7) rectangle (4,2.7);
\foreach \i in {1,2,3} {\draw[black] (\i,1.7)--(\i,2.7);}
\fill[fractionOrange] (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1);
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (4,1);
\foreach \i in {1,...,7} {\draw[black] (0.5*\i,0)--(0.5*\i,1);}
\node[left,text=black] at (0,2.2) {P};
\node[left,text=black] at (0,0.5) {Q};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
